{#
  The control panel's left-hand menu.

  Expects:
    sections      — a list of {label, links: [{route, label, also: [...]}, ...]}
    current_route — app.request.attributes.get('_route'), passed in rather than
                    read here so this partial has no dependency on the request

  ── Why the menu is DATA and not markup ────────────────────────────────────
  Adding a screen becomes one line in an array instead of six lines of nested
  <li>, and — the part that actually matters — the "are we on this page?" test
  is written once and applied identically to every link. Hand-repeated
  highlighting logic is exactly the sort of thing that goes stale on the one
  entry nobody remembered to update.

  `also` lists extra routes belonging to the same entry. An edit screen usually
  has no menu item of its own, but while you are on it the list it came from is
  where you are, and the menu should say so.

  ── The accessibility of "you are here" ────────────────────────────────────
  aria-current="page" is what a screen reader announces; the bold weight and the
  inset bar are what a sighted reader sees. Neither is colour alone, so the cue
  survives both a colour-blind reader and a high-contrast mode.

  Sections are <details open>: they collapse with no JavaScript whatsoever,
  which is what vB3 needed a script for. Open by default because a menu that
  hides itself on arrival is a menu you have to fight.
#}
<nav class="vb-nav" aria-label="Control panel sections">
  {% for section in sections %}
    <details class="vb-nav__section" open>
      <summary>{{ section.label }}</summary>
      <ul>
        {% for link in section.links %}
          {% set is_current = current_route == link.route or current_route in (link.also ?? []) %}
          <li>
            <a href="{{ path(link.route, link.params ?? {}) }}"
               class="{{ is_current ? 'is-current' : '' }}"
               {% if is_current %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
              {{ link.label }}
            </a>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </details>
  {% endfor %}
</nav>
